data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **`ExecuteUpdateAsync`** and **`ExecuteDeleteAsync`** — they translate directly to SQL `UPDATE`/`DELETE` statements without loading entities into memory:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update — single UPDATE statement
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6))
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete — single DELETE statement
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this is fast:**
- Generates a single SQL statement (`UPDATE ... WHERE ...` / `DELETE ... WHERE ...`)
- Zero entities loaded into memory — no tracking, no change detection
- For 500K/2M rows, this is the difference between minutes and seconds

**Caveats:**
- These bypass the change tracker — no `SaveChangesAsync()` needed
- Entity instances already loaded in the same `DbContext` won't reflect the change
- No EF interceptors/events fire for these rows
- If you have a massive delete (2M rows) and want to avoid lock escalation, batch it:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
        .Take(10_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```
